Found while measuring karr #58 (sops 3.13.3, perl 5.36.0). Distinct from #58: this is not a MAC failure, it is silent data loss on the READ path, and it hits ENCRYPTED floats, which #58 correctly excludes from its own scope.

decrypt_value converts the authenticated plaintext with +0.0, producing a bare NV with no PV. Both plaintext emitters then render that NV at 15 significant digits. Measured on a document the real sops wrote, with an ENCRYPTED (ENC[...,type:float]) value 0.30000000000000004:

  sops -d                    -> ratio: 0.30000000000000004
  File::SOPS decrypt_file    -> ratio: 0.3
  sops -d --extract          -> 0.30000000000000004
  File::SOPS extract         -> 0.3

Both formats. The encrypted document itself is fine -- the ciphertext plaintext IS the canonical decimal and the MAC agrees -- so nothing fails; the value simply comes out wrong. Anyone doing decrypt_file, hand-edit, encrypt_file has silently changed the number.

decrypt_file is fixed for free by the emitter change under discussion in #58 (measured: the #58 option (a) prototype makes decrypt_file output 0.30000000000000004 in both formats). extract is NOT: it returns the Perl scalar, and any stringification of that NV by the caller loses the digits. Deciding what extract should return -- an NV, or the canonical decimal as a string like sops prints -- is API-lane policy.

Blocked on #58 landing for the decrypt_file half. The extract half is independent.
