HAELFTE 2 DONE 2026-08-20 (commit f9269d3, file-sops-wire). Option (c) dualvar as decided, built at extract's boundary -- File::SOPS::Encrypted->canonical_float_dualvar, called on the single leaf extract returns. ADR 0010.

Premise re-measured first and CONFIRMED: sops -d --extract 0.30000000000000004 vs our 0.3, both formats, on a document sops wrote.

One refinement to the plan's wording, not a false premise: 'kanonische Dezimalzahl' and 'what sops prints' are the same text only in the middle of the range. value_to_bytes is FormatFloat 'f' (always positional), sops --extract prints the YAML/JSON serialization, which uses an exponent at the extremes -- measured 1e20 -> we say 100000000000000000000, sops says 1e+20; 1e-7 -> 0.0000001 vs 1e-07. Same double, no digits lost either way. Took the canonical decimal as decided; matching sops's spelling would need a SECOND float formatter, which is the duplication this lane exists to prevent. Open as #79 if the maintainer prefers the printed form.

The plan's 'one mechanism with #73' hypothesis was measured and is FALSE in both directions: a dualvar built on the pre-#73 NV inherits IOK and is still an int to detect_type, and a dualvar in the decrypt tree reaches the emitters (Cpanel::JSON::XS quotes it -- an unencrypted JSON float becomes a string, sops -d exit 0; YAML::XS writes its PV, so 1e300 becomes 301 digits). Hence leaf-only, and #73 fixed separately first.

Net: t/24-float-precision.t section 13 (both formats against the binary, plus the boundary and the non-finite cases). Counter-proof with the call backed out: 3 of 34 subtests, 5 assertions. Drift found while measuring: #78, #79, #80.
